We are given the congruence $100x \equiv 1 \pmod{997}$. We want to find the unique three-digit integer $x$ that satisfies this congruence.

We want to find the multiplicative inverse of $100$ modulo $997$. We can use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $a$ and $b$ such that $100a + 997b = 1$.

\begin{align*} 997 &= 9 \cdot 100 + 97 \\ 100 &= 1 \cdot 97 + 3 \\ 97 &= 32 \cdot 3 + 1 \\ 3 &= 3 \cdot 1 + 0 \end{align*}
Now we can work backwards to express 1 as a linear combination of 100 and 997:
\begin{align*} 1 &= 97 - 32 \cdot 3 \\ 1 &= 97 - 32(100 - 1 \cdot 97) \\ 1 &= 97 - 32 \cdot 100 + 32 \cdot 97 \\ 1 &= 33 \cdot 97 - 32 \cdot 100 \\ 1 &= 33(997 - 9 \cdot 100) - 32 \cdot 100 \\ 1 &= 33 \cdot 997 - 297 \cdot 100 - 32 \cdot 100 \\ 1 &= 34 \cdot 997 - 329 \cdot 100 \end{align*}
From the equation $1 = 34 \cdot 997 - 329 \cdot 100$, we see that $-329$ is the coefficient of $100$, meaning $-329$ is a solution to $100x \equiv 1 \pmod{997}$. Since we need a positive solution, we convert $-329$ to a positive equivalent by adding $997$:
\begin{align*} -329 + 997 &= 668 \end{align*}
Thus, the unique three-digit positive integer $x$ that satisfies the congruence $100x \equiv 1 \pmod{997}$ is $\boxed{668}$.

